  "query_preview": "..The Northern Rockies shortwave trough that brought\nthe winds Wednesday continues to push eastward this morning, \nloosening its grip on Arizona. A trailing, very weak trough will \nremain over Arizona today with warm temperatures and much lighter \nwinds. The flow becomes more zonal on Friday, and then increases \nagain over the weekend with the development of a trough over the \nPacific Northwest. Temperatures through Friday will remain about \n5-10 degrees warmer than normal.\n\nAttention over the weekend turns to the increasing winds ahead of\nthe Pacific Northwest trough that digs southward into the Great\nBasin on Sunday. Sunday still looks to be the windier day with the\nsouthward digging trough and tightening gradients, with peak gusts\nin the 35-45 mph range. Areas of critical fire weather are\ncertainly possible both Saturday and Sunday, but the complicating\nfactor over the higher terrain will be rising humidity in response\nto cooler temperatures. The most likely areas to see critical\nconditions are northeastern Arizona on Sunday. Though the trough\npassage through the Great Basin looks mostly dry for us, there are\nsome model runs that are digging it a bit further south and\ngenerating some showers along the AZ/UT border. Not enough\nconfidence to include in the forecast at this point, but something\nto watch. \n\nTemperatures early next week will start off very close to normal,\nbut will gradually rise above normal through the week. Dry\nconditions will persist as Arizona remains under a weak zonal \nflow. The aforementioned Great Basin trough passage will continue\nto bring breezy winds through Monday, but slackening gradients \nthereafter should lead to less wind through the week.",
